From: vgd3d12
Subject: venus: signal D3DKMT fences in order with a queue

A D3D driver over Venus presents allocations whose rendering is on a host queue that Windows' scheduler
cannot see, so it had to wait on the CPU for the GPU before every present. Give it a monitored fence
the GPU signals instead, through private entry points of vkGetDeviceProcAddr:

  vkOpenFenceVGD(device, nt_handle, &fence)   opens a shared monitored fence on our device
  vkCloseFenceVGD(device, fence)
  vkQueueSignalFenceVGD(queue, fence, value)  signals it once the queue's work so far completes

The signal is a timeline packet (VIOGPU_CMD_SUBMIT_TIMELINE, yttrium's kernel driver) on the queue's
ring_idx, whose host fence retires after the queue's earlier work, carrying a vkWaitRingSeqnoMESA for
the queue's last ring submission so the host has executed it first; the GPU signal is queued behind
the packet on our context. The kernel driver retires DMA buffers in order, so later packets on the
context cannot overtake it.

--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device.c
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device.c
@@ -677,6 +677,14 @@
 vn_GetDeviceProcAddr(VkDevice device, const char *pName)
 {
    struct vn_device *dev = vn_device_from_handle(device);
+#ifdef VK_USE_PLATFORM_WIN32_KHR
+   if (!strcmp(pName, "vkOpenFenceVGD"))
+      return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)vn_OpenFenceVGD;
+   if (!strcmp(pName, "vkCloseFenceVGD"))
+      return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)vn_CloseFenceVGD;
+   if (!strcmp(pName, "vkQueueSignalFenceVGD"))
+      return (PFN_vkVoidFunction)vn_QueueSignalFenceVGD;
+#endif
    return vk_device_get_proc_addr(&dev->base.vk, pName);
 }
 
